  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\". The two reply texts are:\n\nText 1: \"Here is an inline ```json {\"a\": 1}``` and then {\"b\": 2}\"\nText 2: \"{\"a\": 1}\"\n\nFor Text 1, the inline triple backticks are not a code fence because they are not at the start of a line, so the object inside them is a top-level object, not a fenced one. The last top-level object is {\"b\": 2}, so the rule requires recover_json_object to return ({\"b\": 2}, ()). For Text 2, the only top-level object is {\"a\": 1}, so the rule requires ({\"a\": 1}, ()). The function, however, treats the inline triple backticks in Text 1 as a code fence because its regex does not require line boundaries, so it returns the fenced object {\"a\": 1} for Text 1, and the same {\"a\": 1} for Text 2. Thus it gives the same answer on a pair the rule requires it to tell apart. I would be wrong if recover_json_object returns different answers for the two texts, or if the documented rule is read as treating any triple backtick span, even inline, as a code fence."
